diff --git a/docs/next/api/bound-interruption.md b/docs/next/api/bound-interruption.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..dcbc105
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/next/api/bound-interruption.md
@@ -0,0 +1,51 @@
+# Bound interruption
+
+Unix servers advertise `ping.capabilities.agent_interrupt_bound` when the native
+writer supports `agent.interrupt_bound`. Windows advertises false. This method
+is independent of request-bound approvals, which remain unsupported.
+
+The request contains `operation_id`, `expected_launch_id`, and
+`expected_owner_epoch`. It accepts only an exact managed direct-process runtime
+whose immutable launch profile has a supported interruption key. The initial
+Codex, Claude, and OpenCode profiles use one Escape byte. Ordinary terminals,
+unsupported profiles, changed owners, and missing launches are not redirected
+to another pane or a shell. Existing terminal controls retain their semantics.
+
+A success result has type `agent_interrupted_bound` and includes the same
+operation, launch and owner epoch, an opaque `receipt_id`, `key: "Escape"`,
+`bytes_written: 1`, and `input_disposition: "written"`. It proves that one byte
+was written, not that the provider cancelled work, approved anything, or exited.
+Escape during an incomplete bracketed paste may be consumed as paste data.
+Only the separate successful-child-wait lifecycle receipt proves process exit.
+
+Control permits depend on permanent process ownership, not prompt readiness.
+The actor serializes the single write with ownership invalidation and revokes
+earlier queued prompt permits. It removes an active bound prompt's remaining
+chunks, including delayed Enter. Its completion reports zero-byte refusal or
+partial-delivery uncertainty according to bytes already written. There is no
+automatic retry, extra Enter, signal escalation, or process replacement.
+Queued-input suppression covers managed bound submissions. Independent legacy
+terminal/raw input retains its existing behavior and is not atomically coordinated
+with this control action; callers must not claim otherwise.
+
+An epoch-local registry reserves an operation before enqueueing. It retains up
+to 4096 operations without eviction. Matching repeated operations return their
+original outcome; a changed identity conflicts; pending/lost acknowledgements
+remain unconfirmed and never dispatch a second write. Exhaustion refuses new
+dispatch with `resource_limit`, a pre-admission no-write refusal. A duplicate
+pending operation remains `delivery_unconfirmed`, which is a different outcome.
+Restart changes owner epoch; stale requests cannot acquire a new
+destination. This is bounded in-memory native evidence, not durable task history.
+The Gateway must persist its own admission and receipt before allowing recovery.
+
+Actor tests cover interrupted partial prompts, revoked queued prompts, blocked
+readiness, and exit before the syscall. Registry/schema tests cover replay,
+capacity and identity. Simulated process proof establishes byte transport only;
+it does not establish real-provider cancellation or mobile integration.
+
+Agent discovery also includes optional `launch_id` and `owner_epoch` on the
+`AgentInfo` returned by list/get and bound lookup. They identify the current
+native directly owned runtime, never its pane, display name or conversation.
+The pair survives readiness/status changes and is omitted for legacy runtimes,
+retired owners and unknown ownership. This observation is not an exit receipt;
+capacity release still requires the separate exact lifecycle evidence.
diff --git a/docs/next/api/bound-lifecycle.md b/docs/next/api/bound-lifecycle.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..6c22358
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/next/api/bound-lifecycle.md
@@ -0,0 +1,68 @@
+# Bound assistant lifecycle evidence
+
+These Unix-only JSON methods describe directly owned child processes. They do not
+prove task completion, agent-native approval identity, or descendant cleanup.
+Windows does not advertise the capabilities.
+
+`ping.result.capabilities` advertises `agent_lifecycle_bound` and an opaque
+`owner_epoch`. Every supervisor process creates a fresh epoch. Successful
+`agent.start_bound` replies additionally carry this epoch beside the launch and
+operation IDs. Only the canonical native executable for the requested agent on
+the server's trusted PATH is eligible. Shell overrides and script wrappers are
+unsupported; ordinary agent APIs retain their existing behavior.
+
+The startup receipt also carries `agent_kind`, the validated declared launch
+profile. Its nested `agent` remains observational: immediately after creation,
+`agent.agent` may be absent, `agent_status` unknown, and `launch_pending` true.
+Validate the declared profile and immutable receipt first, then poll readiness
+through `agent.get_bound`; creation alone never proves interactive readiness.
+
+Call `agent.lifecycle_bound` with `expected_launch_id` and
+`expected_owner_epoch`. Its `agent_bound_lifecycle` result includes `launch_id`,
+the current `owner_epoch`, and `state`:
+
+- `live`: this supervisor owns the exact unreaped generation.
+- `exited`: its child waiter observed successful exit/reaping. An immutable
+  `receipt_id` is present. Nonzero exit is still exit, not successful work.
+- `unknown`: no retained matching proof is available. This includes epoch
+  mismatch, restart, imported ownership without a waiter, wait failure, and
+  tombstone eviction. No exit receipt is present.
+
+The waiter records exit before delivering pane events. Removing or replacing a
+pane cannot discard that receipt. Invalidating input ownership, requesting
+shutdown, missing a pane, or observing an idle screen never establishes exit.
+
+The in-memory registry retains at most 1024 unreaped or uncertain launches and
+4096 exit tombstones. It refuses new bound launches before spawning when owner
+capacity is full. Live and uncertain owners are never evicted. Oldest exit
+tombstones are evicted by count; their later queries become unknown. There is no
+automatic force release or filesystem cleanup.
+
+A start refusal before any spawn attempt adds a top-level `start_receipt` beside
+the existing `id` and `error` fields:
+
+```json
+{
+  "id": "request",
+  "error": {"code": "resource_limit", "message": "native lifecycle capacity exhausted before spawn"},
+  "start_receipt": {
+    "operation_id": "start-operation",
+    "owner_epoch": "opaque-epoch",
+    "receipt_id": "opaque-receipt",
+    "effect": "not_started"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Failures after spawn was attempted carry no such receipt, even if an OS error
+suggests startup failed. The supervisor remembers up to 4096 start operation IDs
+per incarnation without eviction. Reusing an ID, or exhausting this operation
+capacity, returns `delivery_unconfirmed` without starting a process or minting a
+new refusal receipt. This prevents a later rejection from being mistaken for
+proof that an earlier request never started. Clients must retain their original
+receipt and never automatically retry uncertain startup. Operation receipt
+lookup is not implemented.
+
+Consumers release execution reservations only from an exact matching no-process
+receipt or retained exit proof. Unknown observations preserve reservations.
+Release does not stop a process, remove a workspace, or complete a task.
diff --git a/docs/next/api/bound-reporting.md b/docs/next/api/bound-reporting.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..8b0edf7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/next/api/bound-reporting.md
@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@
+# Managed Codex reporting
+
+`agent_reporting_mcp_codex` advertises the Unix native configuration adapter.
+It is not a claim that every installed Codex release supports the configuration,
+that MCP initialization succeeded, or that a result was submitted.
+
+`agent.start_bound` accepts optional `reporting_mcp: {executable, sha256}` with a
+private `work_context_file`, only for the canonical Codex profile without custom
+arguments. The reporter must be the digest-named native executable in the context
+file's sibling `reporting-binaries` directory. The adapter verifies its bounded
+bytes and private mode, then appends a generated per-invocation config override.
+This uses the trusted local installation boundary, not signed attestation against
+another process running as the same user.
+
+The unique server namespace is `muqun_reporting_` plus an operation-derived suffix.
+The only enabled tools are `context`, `submit_result`, and `result_receipt`, each
+with approval mode `approve`; other tools default to `prompt`. The fixed stdio
+command is the pinned executable with `work reporting-mcp`. Only the context path
+is explicitly forwarded through `MUQUN_WORK_CONTEXT_FILE`. No token is in argv.
+No global configuration, authentication, general shell approval policy or sandbox
+permission is changed. Codex strict config and required MCP startup fail closed
+when unsupported. The additive start receipt `reporting_mcp: "codex_stdio_v1"`
+confirms this configuration was applied to the created child, not readiness.
+
+Other agents retain their existing explicit reporting path; this capability does
+not grant them automatic reporting permission. A missing MCP tool in managed Codex
+must produce a reporting-unavailable explanation, never silent shell escalation.
+The reporting adapter delegates existing Gateway task/attempt/grant/revision and
+immutable-artifact checks. Human review, merge, publication, and interruption are
+separate actions and are not exposed by these reporting tools.
+
+Verified locally against Codex CLI 0.154.0: generated config parses into the exact
+transport, environment allowlist, tool allowlist and per-tool approval values.
+Model-driven no-prompt reporting still requires the paired integration gate.
+Configuration fields follow the [official MCP documentation](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/extend/mcp?surface=cli)
+and [configuration reference](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/config-file/config-reference).
+
+## Managed profile discovery
+
+On each explicit ping, optional `capabilities.bound_agent_kinds` lists profiles
+that pass the same canonical PATH/native executable admission check used for
+bound startup. An empty list means no eligible installed profiles; absent means
+unknown compatibility. It is not a promise of readiness or task completion.
+Dispatch revalidates after executable upgrades or replacement. Ordinary terminal
+and legacy agent catalogs are unaffected; Codex reporting support remains the
+separate reporting capability.
